<GuideLayout
  title="Dead Pixel vs Stuck Pixel — How to Tell Them Apart"
  description="A dead pixel receives no power and stays black on every fill. A stuck sub-pixel is powered but jammed, so it shows as a coloured dot on black and can sometimes be freed. How to tell which you have, and how to rule out dust."
  heading="Dead pixel vs stuck pixel"
  lede="They look similar in a photograph and behave completely differently in practice. One is permanent; the other is sometimes fixable in a few minutes. Three fills will tell you which you have."
  updated="2026-08-27"
  slug="dead-vs-stuck-pixel"
>
  <Card surface="soft" elevation="e1" padding="lg">
    <p class="text-body-sm font-medium text-ink">In short</p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-body-sm text-body">
      A <strong>dead</strong> pixel gets no power at all, so it stays black on
      every colour &mdash; including white &mdash; and no software can revive it.
      A <strong>stuck</strong> sub-pixel is powered but jammed in one state, so
      it appears as a coloured dot against black and can sometimes be freed by
      rapid colour cycling. View <a href="/test/white">white</a>, then
      <a href="/test/black">black</a>: black-on-white means dead, coloured-on-black
      means stuck.
    </p>
  </Card>

  <h2>What is physically different</h2>
  <p>
    Every pixel on an LCD or OLED panel is three sub-pixels &mdash; one red, one
    green, one blue &mdash; each driven by its own thin-film transistor. Nearly
    every fault you will ever see is a failure of one of those transistors, and
    it fails in one of two ways.
  </p>
  <p>
    A <strong>dead</strong> pixel&rsquo;s transistor receives no power. It cannot
    be told to do anything, so it renders nothing, so it is black regardless of
    what the rest of the screen is doing. That is why white is the fill that
    finds it: against a bright field a black dot is obvious, and against black it
    is invisible. There is no software fix, because there is nothing to send a
    signal to.
  </p>
  <p>
    A <strong>stuck</strong> sub-pixel is the opposite: it is powered, but its
    transistor is jammed at one value and stops responding to changes. It shows
    as a small coloured dot &mdash; red, green, blue, or a mix &mdash; and it is
    most visible on black, because that is the one fill where the rest of the
    screen is dark and it is not. Because the circuit is live, rapidly exercising
    it sometimes unsticks it. That is what the
    <a href="/fixer">stuck pixel fixer</a> does.
  </p>
  <p>
    A <strong>hot</strong> pixel is the extreme case of stuck: all three
    sub-pixels jammed on, so the pixel is permanently white. On a black field it
    is the brightest thing on the screen. Hot pixels rarely respond to cycling.
  </p>

  <h2>Most &ldquo;dead pixels&rdquo; are dead sub-pixels</h2>
  <p>
    This is the single most common misreading. If one sub-pixel of three fails
    dark, the pixel still lights &mdash; just with a channel missing. A pixel
    with a dead red sub-pixel shows cyan where it should show white. So it reads
    as a faint <em>coloured</em> dot on a white field, not a black one, and it is
    far more common than a genuinely dead whole pixel. If you see a tinted dot on
    white, count the channels rather than assuming the worst.
  </p>

  <h2>The decision procedure</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <a href="/test/white">View white</a>. Anything dark here is losing a
      channel or all three.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/test/black">View black</a>. Anything bright here is stuck on.
    </li>
    <li>
      View each primary in turn &mdash; <a href="/test/red">red</a>,
      <a href="/test/green">green</a>, <a href="/test/blue">blue</a> &mdash; to
      identify <em>which</em> sub-pixel is at fault. A dot that stays dark on red
      but lights on green and blue has a dead red sub-pixel.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The <a href="/test/sequence">guided run</a> walks all twelve fills in this
    order and logs where you tap, which is worth doing if you might need the
    evidence later.
  </p>

  <div class="guide-table">
    <table>
      <caption>How each fault reads on each fill</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Fault</th>
          <th scope="col">On white</th>
          <th scope="col">On black</th>
          <th scope="col">On red / green / blue</th>
          <th scope="col">Fixable?</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Dead pixel</th>
          <td>Black dot</td>
          <td>Invisible</td>
          <td>Black on all three</td>
          <td>No</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Dead sub-pixel</th>
          <td>Faintly tinted dot</td>
          <td>Invisible</td>
          <td>Black on one, normal on the other two</td>
          <td>No</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Stuck sub-pixel</th>
          <td>Usually invisible</td>
          <td>Coloured dot</td>
          <td>Bright on its own colour, coloured on the others</td>
          <td>Sometimes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Hot pixel</th>
          <td>Invisible</td>
          <td>White dot</td>
