          <td>Rarely</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Two things that are not pixel faults</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Dust or a smudge on the surface.</strong> It sits on top of the glass,
    so it sits on top of the reflection rather than inside the image, it usually
    has a soft irregular edge, and it moves or disappears when you wipe with a
    dry microfibre cloth. Check this before anything else &mdash; it is the
    commonest false alarm, and it costs nothing to rule out.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>A bright spot under the polariser.</strong> Debris trapped inside the
    panel stack, or a pressure mark, produces a pale blob that does not move when
    wiped. Tell it from a pixel fault by shape and scale: a stuck or hot pixel is
    hard-edged and exactly one pixel across, so it stays crisp as you lean in,
    while a polariser defect is a slightly larger fuzzy patch with no sharp
    boundary. Neither responds to colour cycling, but they are described
    differently in a warranty claim, so the distinction is worth making.
  </p>

  <h2>What to do next</h2>
  <p>
    If it is stuck, try the <a href="/fixer">fixer</a> &mdash; it costs a few
    minutes and occasionally works. If it is dead, or if cycling does nothing,
    the question becomes whether the panel qualifies for a return or a claim.
    Record the faults with the <a href="/test/sequence">guided run</a>, then read
    <a href="/guides/dead-pixel-warranty-policies">how to find the warranty
    policy that actually applies</a>.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
